[
    {
        "content": "<p>I would like to have a type to describe (plane: left child is different from right child) binary trees that are possibly infinite (and labels on the nodes, but this is not a problem).</p>\n<p>In Rocq I can do this with a <code>CoInductive</code> type, but my understanding is that Lean does not have coinductive datatypes - the <code>coinductive</code> keyword only describes predicates.</p>\n<p>If I write something like</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">inductive</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">tree</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Leaf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">tree</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span>\n<span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Node</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">l</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">tree</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">tree</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">):</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">tree</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>my trees are finite, they cannot have infinitely long branches; the corresponding coinductive type in Rocq has both finite and infinite trees.</p>\n<p>Is there no way in Lean to define the equivalent finite-or-infinite trees? (I know writing functions with coinductive types in Rocq becomes significantly more difficult, but I'd still like to know the answer)</p>",
        "id": 592118749,
        "sender_full_name": "Philippe Duchon",
        "timestamp": 1777570369
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>in this case you can use <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=PFunctor.M#doc\">docs#PFunctor.M</a> defined in mathlib</p>",
        "id": 592120195,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1777570786
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks! I have not much of an idea of what a functor is, but at least I know where to look.</p>",
        "id": 592161218,
        "sender_full_name": "Philippe Duchon",
        "timestamp": 1777584471
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"816344\">@Aaron Liu</span>, do you think you could write a longer docstring for that, or maybe even add an example instantiation?</p>",
        "id": 592161797,
        "sender_full_name": "Eric Wieser",
        "timestamp": 1777584720
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"726077\">@Philippe Duchon</span> you may also want to check the implementation of <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Std.Stream#doc\">docs#Std.Stream</a>:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">class</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Stream</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">stream</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">value</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">outParam</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">max</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">next?</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">stream</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Option</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">value</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">×</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">stream</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 592413647,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1777703338
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>If we write <code>instance Stream α yielder</code> (because it's a typeclass not an implementation)<br>\nthis is similar to the API you'd imagine for </p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">coinductive</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">yielder</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Next?</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Option</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">×</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">yielder</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">yielder</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 592414168,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1777703772
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>or for </p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">coinductive</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">yielder</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kn\">where</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">None</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">()</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">yielder</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Cons</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">×</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">yielder</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">yielder</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 592414220,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1777703837
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The functors Aaron is talking about are shapes like <code>Option (α × _)</code> which define the shape of the (co)recursion and (co)induction</p>",
        "id": 592414316,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris C",
        "timestamp": 1777703917
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"310045\">@Eric Wieser</span> <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/40677\">https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/40677</a> here's a proposed example added to the docstring</p>",
        "id": 603856910,
        "sender_full_name": "nrs",
        "timestamp": 1781630299
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I also needed possibly infinite trees for one of my projects and ended up developing a small standalone library for them: <a href=\"https://github.com/monsterkrampe/Possibly-Infinite-Trees\">https://github.com/monsterkrampe/Possibly-Infinite-Trees</a><br>\nThe trees are not binary but there is at least a version enforcing finite degree. Also defining binary (possibly) infinite trees should work in a very similar fashion.</p>\n<p>The library also (re)introduces (possibly) infinite lists heavily inspired by mathlib's <code>Stream'</code>.<br>\nBack in the day, I felt like I don't want to include mathlib as I do not need most of its machinery and wanted to understand for myself how things could be done. By now I feel like it might make sense to backport a few things but I do not know to which extent this is helpful <span aria-label=\"sweat smile\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f605\" role=\"img\" title=\"sweat smile\">:sweat_smile:</span></p>",
        "id": 613459810,
        "sender_full_name": "Lukas Gerlach",
        "timestamp": 1785340896
    }
]